<!DOCTYPE PubmedArticleSet PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD PubMedArticle, 1st January 2025//EN" "https://dtd.nlm.nih.gov/ncbi/pubmed/out/pubmed_250101.dtd">
<PubmedArticleSet>
<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Curated"><PMID Version="1">42594202</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>08</Month><Day>13</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>08</Month><Day>18</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1095-9203</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>393</Volume><Issue>6812</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Aug</Month><Day>13</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Science (New York, N.Y.)</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Science</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>A thalamocortical circuit encoding deviations from sensory history.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>eaeg4720</StartPage><MedlinePgn>eaeg4720</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1126/science.aeg4720</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Prior expectations guide attention and support perceptual filtering during decision-making. In mice performing a visual discrimination task, choices depended on trial-by-trial differences between consecutive stimuli (|&#x394;Dir|). We hypothesized that thalamic lateral posterior (LP; rodent pulvinar) projections to prefrontal areas such as the anterior cingulate cortex (ACC), previously implicated in selective attention and predictive processing, could support history-dependent evaluation of current sensory evidence. In this work, we report that optogenetic manipulations of LP-ACC axons disrupted history-dependent evaluation of current sensory evidence, producing |&#x394;Dir|-dependent choice biases. Two-photon imaging showed that LP-ACC axons represented stimuli along a task-dependent low-dimensional curved manifold whose geometry scaled with |&#x394;Dir|, emphasizing larger deviations from recent evidence. These findings identify the LP-ACC as a thalamocortical pathway that implements a contrastive, history-referenced representation of sensory evidence during decisions.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Leow</LastName><ForeName>Yi Ning</ForeName><Initials>YN</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-4600-5918</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>The Picower Institute for Learning and Memory (PILM), Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT), Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Brain and Cognitive Sciences (BCS), MIT, Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Institute of Molecular and Cell Biology (IMCB), Agency for Science, Technology and Research (A*STAR), Singapore.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Natesan</LastName><ForeName>Arundhati</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0009-0007-7721-8496</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>The Picower Institute for Learning and Memory (PILM), Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT), Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Barlowe</LastName><ForeName>Alexandria</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>The Picower Institute for Learning and Memory (PILM), Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT), Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>&#xc4;hrlund-Richter</LastName><ForeName>Sofie</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-7845-7831</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>The Picower Institute for Learning and Memory (PILM), Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT), Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Luo</LastName><ForeName>Tianyu Cindy</ForeName><Initials>TC</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-8252-9092</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Brain and Cognitive Sciences (BCS), MIT, Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Jazayeri</LastName><ForeName>Mehrdad</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-9764-6961</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Brain and Cognitive Sciences (BCS), MIT, Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>McGovern Institute of Brain Research (MIBR), MIT, Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Sur</LastName><ForeName>Mriganka</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-2442-5671</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>The Picower Institute for Learning and Memory (PILM), Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT), Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Brain and Cognitive Sciences (BCS), MIT, Cambridge, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>08</Month><Day>13</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>Science</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>0404511</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0036-8075</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000818" MajorTopicYN="N">Animals</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D008297" MajorTopicYN="N">Male</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D051379" MajorTopicYN="N">Mice</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D001288" MajorTopicYN="N">Attention</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D001369" MajorTopicYN="N">Axons</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000502" MajorTopicYN="N">physiology</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D002755" MajorTopicYN="Y">Choice Behavior</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D003657" MajorTopicYN="Y">Decision Making</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D006179" MajorTopicYN="Y">Gyrus Cinguli</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000502" MajorTopicYN="N">physiology</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D009434" MajorTopicYN="N">Neural Pathways</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000502" MajorTopicYN="N">physiology</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D062308" MajorTopicYN="N">Optogenetics</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D020649" MajorTopicYN="Y">Pulvinar</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000502" MajorTopicYN="N">physiology</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D014796" MajorTopicYN="Y">Visual Perception</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000502" MajorTopicYN="N">physiology</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D008810" MajorTopicYN="N">Mice, Inbred C57BL</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D008822" MajorTopicYN="N">Mice, Transgenic</DescriptorName></MeshHeading></MeshHeadingList></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>8</Month><Day>13</Day><Hour>21</Hour><Minute>32</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>8</Month><Day>13</Day><Hour>21</Hour><Minute>31</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>8</Month><Day>13</Day><Hour>14</Hour><Minute>13</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>ppublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">42594202</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1126/science.aeg4720</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">42379409</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>07</Month><Day>31</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>07</Month><Day>31</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1095-9572</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>338</Volume><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Sep</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>NeuroImage</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Neuroimage</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Hysteresis effects in visual and auditory perception and the comparison of underlying neural mechanisms - an EEG study.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>122086</StartPage><MedlinePgn>122086</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.neuroimage.2026.122086</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1053-8119(26)00401-5</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Perceptual hysteresis-the tendency to maintain a current percept even as sensory input shifts towards an alternative interpretation-provides a useful model for studying how recent perceptual history stabilises ongoing perception. The present EEG study, to our best knowledge, provides the first side-by-side examination of the neural correlates of visual and auditory hysteresis within a common experimental and analytical framework. In two experiments, participants viewed ambiguous Necker cubes or listened to ambiguous Shepard tones. In both modalities, stimuli were presented either in a sequential order designed to induce hysteresis or in a randomised order serving as a baseline condition. Behavioural analyses revealed robust order-dependent perceptual biases in both experiments, with hysteresis effects being markedly stronger in the sequential than in the randomised condition, and, in general, more pronounced in the auditory task. This pattern is a central contribution of the present work, as it shows that ordered stimulus presentation induces a stronger history-dependent stabilisation of perception than the local micro-sequences present in random stimulus sequences. At the neural level, inter-trial coherence (ITC) analyses revealed hysteresis-related differences in both modalities, but the oscillatory profiles differed between tasks. In the visual experiment, perception of sequential orders was associated primarily with enhanced alpha and beta band ITC over posterior and frontal electrode sites, without corresponding differences in oscillatory power. In the auditory experiment, hysteresis-related ITC differences were more extensive, including fronto-central alpha band and sustained right-posterior beta band effects, and were accompanied by robust power differences: The baseline condition showed stronger posterior alpha power, whereas the hysteresis condition showed temporally sustained frontal beta power increases. Supplementary source analyses provided supportive evidence for a bilateral involvement of dorsolateral prefrontal and inferior frontal regions. Together, these findings point to partly convergent fronto-posterior involvement in visual and auditory hysteresis, but also suggest modality-specific involvement of sensory-association, attentional, and working-memory-related processes.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Redepenning</LastName><ForeName>Tim</ForeName><Initials>T</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Bielefeld University, Faculty of Psychology and Sports Science, Universit&#xe4;tsstra&#xdf;e 25, Bielefeld, 33615, Germany. Electronic address: tim.redepenning@uni-bielefeld.de.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Porta-Garc&#xed;a</LastName><ForeName>Miguel &#xc1;ngel</ForeName><Initials>M&#xc1;</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centro de Investigaci&#xf3;n e Innovaci&#xf3;n en Tecnolog&#xed;as de la Informaci&#xf3;n y Comunicaci&#xf3;n (INFOTEC), Av. San Fernando 37. Col. Toriello Guerra CP 14050, Tlalpan, Ciudad de M&#xe9;xico, M&#xe9;xico. Electronic address: maporta@gmail.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Sch&#xfc;tz</LastName><ForeName>Christoph</ForeName><Initials>C</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Bielefeld University, Faculty of Psychology and Sports Science, Universit&#xe4;tsstra&#xdf;e 25, Bielefeld, 33615, Germany; Bielefeld University, CITEC, Center for Cognitive Interaction Technology, Inspiration 1, Bielefeld, 33619, Germany. Electronic address: christoph.schuetz@uni-bielefeld.de.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>06</Month><Day>30</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>Neuroimage</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>9215515</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>1053-8119</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D006801" MajorTopicYN="N">Humans</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D001307" MajorTopicYN="Y">Auditory Perception</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000502" MajorTopicYN="N">physiology</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D005260" MajorTopicYN="N">Female</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D014796" MajorTopicYN="Y">Visual Perception</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000502" MajorTopicYN="N">physiology</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D004569" MajorTopicYN="N">Electroencephalography</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000379" MajorTopicYN="N">methods</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D008297" MajorTopicYN="N">Male</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000328" MajorTopicYN="N">Adult</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D055815" MajorTopicYN="N">Young Adult</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000161" MajorTopicYN="N">Acoustic Stimulation</DescriptorName></MeshHeading></MeshHeadingList><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Ambiguous figures</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Bistable perception</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Hysteresis</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Inter-trial coherence</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Phase coherence</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Power spectral density</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Serial dependence</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Source localisation</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2026</Year><Month>2</Month><Day>10</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2026</Year><Month>6</Month><Day>29</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>6</Month><Day>29</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>7</Month><Day>31</Day><Hour>6</Hour><Minute>36</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>7</Month><Day>1</Day><Hour>0</Hour><Minute>35</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>6</Month><Day>30</Day><Hour>19</Hour><Minute>30</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>ppublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">42379409</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.neuroimage.2026.122086</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">S1053-8119(26)00401-5</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">38253526</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2024</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>24</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>09</Month><Day>22</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2041-1723</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>15</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2024</Year><Month>Jan</Month><Day>22</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Nature communications</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Nat Commun</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Trial-history biases in evidence accumulation can give rise to apparent lapses in decision-making.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>662</StartPage><MedlinePgn>662</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">662</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/s41467-024-44880-5</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Trial history biases and lapses are two of the most common suboptimalities observed during perceptual decision-making. These suboptimalities are routinely assumed to arise from distinct processes. However, previous work has suggested that they covary in their prevalence and that their proposed neural substrates overlap. Here we demonstrate that during decision-making, history biases and apparent lapses can both arise from a common cognitive process that is optimal under mistaken beliefs that the world is changing i.e. nonstationary. This corresponds to an accumulation-to-bound model with history-dependent updates to the initial state of the accumulator. We test our model's predictions about the relative prevalence of history biases and lapses, and show that they are robustly borne out in two distinct decision-making datasets of male rats, including data from a novel reaction time task. Our model improves the ability to precisely predict decision-making dynamics within and across trials, by positing a process through which agents can generate quasi-stochastic choices.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2024. The Author(s).</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gupta</LastName><ForeName>Diksha</ForeName><Initials>D</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-8811-3311</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Princeton Neuroscience Institute, Princeton University, Princeton, NJ, USA. diksha.gupta@ucl.ac.uk.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Sainsbury Wellcome Centre, University College London, London, UK. diksha.gupta@ucl.ac.uk.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>DePasquale</LastName><ForeName>Brian</ForeName><Initials>B</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Princeton Neuroscience Institute, Princeton University, Princeton, NJ, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Biomedical Engineering, Boston University, Boston, MA, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kopec</LastName><ForeName>Charles D</ForeName><Initials>CD</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Princeton Neuroscience Institute, Princeton University, Princeton, NJ, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Brody</LastName><ForeName>Carlos D</ForeName><Initials>CD</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-4201-561X</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Princeton Neuroscience Institute, Princeton University, Princeton, NJ, USA. brody@princeton.edu.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Howard Hughes Medical Institute, Princeton University, Princeton, NJ, USA. brody@princeton.edu.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><DataBankList CompleteYN="Y"><DataBank><DataBankName>figshare</DataBankName><AccessionNumberList><AccessionNumber>10.6084/m9.figshare.24113793</AccessionNumber></AccessionNumberList></DataBank></DataBankList><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><GrantID>R01 MH108358</GrantID><Acronym>MH</Acronym><Agency>NIMH NIH HHS</Agency><Country>United States</Country></Grant></GrantList><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D052061">Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D013485">Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2024</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>22</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>England</Country><MedlineTA>Nat Commun</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>101528555</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>2041-1723</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><CommentsCorrectionsList><CommentsCorrections RefType="UpdateOf"><RefSource>bioRxiv. 2023 Feb 01:2023.01.18.524599. doi: 10.1101/2023.01.18.524599.</RefSource><PMID Version="1">36778392</PMID></CommentsCorrections></CommentsCorrectionsList><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D008297" MajorTopicYN="N">Male</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000818" MajorTopicYN="N">Animals</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D051381" MajorTopicYN="N">Rats</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D015982" MajorTopicYN="N">Bias</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D008606" MajorTopicYN="Y">Mental Processes</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D011930" MajorTopicYN="N">Reaction Time</DescriptorName></MeshHeading></MeshHeadingList><CoiStatement>The authors declare no competing interests.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2023</Year><Month>1</Month><Day>22</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2024</Year><Month>1</Month><Day>4</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2024</Year><Month>1</Month><Day>24</Day><Hour>6</Hour><Minute>43</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2024</Year><Month>1</Month><Day>23</Day><Hour>0</Hour><Minute>42</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2024</Year><Month>1</Month><Day>22</Day><Hour>23</Hour><Minute>32</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pmc-release"><Year>2024</Year><Month>1</Month><Day>22</Day></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>epublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">38253526</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC10803295</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/s41467-024-44880-5</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">10.1038/s41467-024-44880-5</ArticleId></ArticleIdList><ReferenceList><Reference><Citation>Cho R, et al. Mechanisms underlying dependencies of performance on stimulus history in a two-alternative forced-choice task. Cogn. Affect. Behav. Neurosci. 2002;2:283&#x2013;299. doi: 10.3758/CABN.2.4.283.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3758/CABN.2.4.283</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">12641174</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Gold J, Law C, Connolly P, Bennur S. The relative influences of priors and sensory evidence on an oculomotor decision variable during perceptual learning. J. Neurophysiol. 2008;100:2653&#x2013;2668. doi: 10.1152/jn.90629.2008.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1152/jn.90629.2008</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC2585410</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">18753326</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Busse L, et al. The detection of visual contrast in the behaving mouse. J. Neurosci. 2011;31:11351&#x2013;11361. doi: 10.1523/JNEUROSCI.6689-10.2011.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1523/JNEUROSCI.6689-10.2011</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC6623377</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">21813694</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Carandini M, Churchland A. Probing perceptual decisions in rodents. Nat. Neurosci. 2013;16:824&#x2013;831. doi: 1